Scott's Theorem 4.4 states that, starting from a continuous lattice \(D_0\) and a chosen projection of \(D_0\) into \([D_0\to D_0]\), the tower \(D_{n+1}=[D_n\to D_n]\) with \(i_{n+1}(x)=i_n\circ x\circ j_n\) and \(j_{n+1}(x')=j_n\circ x'\circ i_n\) has inverse limit \(D_\infty\) which is a continuous lattice and is homeomorphic to \([D_\infty\to D_\infty]\) by \(i_\infty(x)=\bigsqcup_n(i_{n\infty}\circ x_{n+1}\circ j_{\infty n})\) and \(j_\infty(f)=\bigsqcup_n i_{(n+1)\infty}(j_{\infty n}\circ f\circ i_{n\infty})\); the preamble also says \(D_\infty\) is the direct limit along the \(i_n\) and that \(i_n(f(x))=i_{n+1}(f)(i_n(x))\). The proof reduces \(j_\infty(i_\infty(x))\) to \(\bigsqcup_n i_{(n+1)\infty}(x_{n+1})\) by collapsing a double monotone supremum, then reduces \(i_\infty(j_\infty(f))\) to \(\bigsqcup_n(i_{n\infty}\circ j_{\infty n})\circ f\circ\bigsqcup_n(i_{n\infty}\circ j_{\infty n})\) by continuity of \(f\), and invokes the remark after 4.2 that this supremum is the identity. The AI English of `theorem_4_4` establishes the continuous-lattice claim and a homeomorphism between \(D_\infty\) with the inverse-limit subspace topology induced from the product and \([D_\infty\to D_\infty]\) with the pointwise topology of Definition 3.1, by induction on levels (`theorem_3_3_isContinuousLattice`), `proposition_4_1`, and the [source-topology homeomorphism](1972_sourceTopology.md). `theorem_4_4_inverses` records the two identities \(j_\infty\circ i_\infty=\mathrm{id}\) and \(i_\infty\circ j_\infty=\mathrm{id}\) for `projInfInf` and `embInfInf`. `theorem_4_4_orderIso` adds that the same pair is an order isomorphism because both maps are monotone. The Scott-topology packaging of the same pair is [scottTopology](1972_scottTopology.md). The formulas for \(i_\infty\) and \(j_\infty\) agree with the note. Absent from the AI English are the direct-limit claim, the recursive formulas for \(i_{n+1}\) and \(j_{n+1}\), the application identity, the double-supremum collapse, and the identity \(\mathrm{id}=\bigsqcup_n(i_{n\infty}\circ j_{\infty n})\). **Theorem:** partial — the continuous-lattice claim and the homeomorphism are present, while the direct-limit claim and the levelwise formulas are absent. **Proof:** partial — mutual inversion is named, while Scott's supremum reductions are absent and the topology transport is the source-topology argument.
